{{define "title"}}Example MPP files - open a sample MS Project plan | MPP Viewer{{end}}

{{define "main"}}
<main class="page page--text centered">
  <div class="page__head">
    <h1>Example plans</h1>
    <a class="btn btn--primary btn--inline" href="/">Open your own file</a>
  </div>

  <p class="lead">
    Real MS&nbsp;Project schedules you can open right now - no file of your own, no signup.
    Each one shows the Gantt chart, the task table and the dependencies exactly as the viewer draws them.
  </p>

  <ul class="cards">
    {{range .Form}}
      <li class="card">
        <a class="card__name" href="/example/{{.Name}}">{{.Label}}</a>
        <div class="card__meta">
          <span>{{.Note}}</span>
          <code>{{.FileName}}</code>
        </div>
      </li>
    {{end}}
  </ul>

  <section class="prose">
<h2>What these samples are for</h2>

<p>
  Two situations bring people here. Either you want to see what a Microsoft Project schedule
  looks like before you have one of your own to open, or you have a file that matters and would
  rather watch a stranger's plan render correctly before handing over yours. Both are reasonable,
  and both take one click from here.
</p>

<p>
  The three plans below are not decoration. Each one exists because it breaks something that
  commonly goes wrong when a <code>.mpp</code> file is read outside Microsoft Project.
</p>

<h2>What each one demonstrates</h2>

<ul class="facts">
  <li>
    <strong>Office fit-out</strong> - phases inside phases, a full set of dependencies and a
    marked critical path. This is the shape of a normal construction or delivery schedule, and
    the one to open if you want to know whether the hierarchy and the dependency arrows survive
    the trip out of Microsoft Project.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Viaduct</strong> - task names in Cyrillic. Non-Latin text is where cheap converters
    fall over, usually producing rows of question marks, because the older <code>.mpp</code>
    generations store text in a way that punishes a lazy reader. Open it and the names are simply
    there.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Roadworks</strong> - a Project <code>.xml</code> file rather than a binary
    <code>.mpp</code>. Worth knowing about: if a sender cannot get you an <code>.mpp</code>, this
    is the format to ask for, and it opens here identically.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>A note on what a sample can tell you</h2>

<p>
  These are small plans, built to exercise the reader rather than to impress anyone: real
  structure and real dependencies, invented content, no client data. A sample proves that
  hierarchy, dates, dependencies and text render properly. It cannot prove anything about how a
  fifteen-hundred-task programme behaves, which is a fair question - the honest answer is to open
  yours and see, and since nothing is stored, that costs you nothing but the time it takes.
</p>

<p>
  There is no download link on this page, deliberately. The samples open in the viewer; they are
  not offered as files to take away. If you came looking for an example <code>.mpp</code> to test
  some other tool with, the file already sitting in your inbox is a better test than anything we
  could hand you.
</p>

<h2>Check the difference before you upload anything</h2>

<p>
  This page has an unusual advantage over the rest of the argument: you do not have to believe
  any of it. Most free viewers for this format render a plan as pages you scroll and download as
  a PDF - a picture of the schedule rather than the schedule. The difference sounds small
  written down and is obvious the moment you touch it, so open the <a href="/example/office-fit-out">office fit-out sample</a> and try
  the four things a page image cannot do.
</p>

<p>
  Switch the scale from day to quarter and back. Collapse a phase and expand it again. Click a
  task and read its predecessors, resources and notes in the panel. Trace a dependency arrow to
  where it lands. That is roughly ten seconds of work, it costs nothing, and it is a far better
  test than any description - including this one - of whether this is the kind of tool you need.
</p>

<p>
  Then, if it is, open your own file. Same behaviour, no daily counter running down, and nothing
  kept afterwards.
</p>

<h2>Common questions</h2>

<dl class="faq">
  <dt>Can I download these as .mpp files?</dt>
  <dd>No. The samples open directly in the viewer; they are not offered as files to download. If you need a real .mpp to test with, the plan someone sent you is the best one to use - opening it here costs nothing and stores nothing.</dd>

  <dt>Why look at a sample before my own plan?</dt>
  <dd>To see how a schedule is drawn before handing over a file that may be confidential. The samples cover the three things that usually break elsewhere: deep nesting, non-Latin task names and the XML flavour of the format.</dd>

  <dt>Are these real schedules?</dt>
  <dd>They are small plans built to exercise the reader - real structure, real dependencies, invented content. No client data appears in them.</dd>

</dl>

  </section>
</main>

